─── app/services/scene_manual_revision_service.py:204-206 ───
[bug · medium] Concurrency / lost-update race: this read-check-then-`bag.row_version += 1` (ORM
flush on commit) is not the authoritative guard used elsewhere in the codebase.
`ingest_service.update_submission` explicitly documents (the `F2` comment) that under true
concurrency the loser session can flush its content and then hit a `rowcount`/`StaleDataError`
(→500) instead of a clean 409, and that the fix is a *single* guarded `UPDATE ... WHERE
row_version=:expected ... SET row_version=row_version+1`. Here two same-`row_version` writers to the
same bag can both pass the `if bag.row_version != expected` check before either commits, so one's
revision can be silently lost (or surface as 500). Consider issuing one guarded UPDATE for the
availability/row_version change (and writing the MANUAL evidence + audit) so the losing writer
deterministically gets 409. Same pattern applies in `withdraw_slice` below.



─── app/services/scene_manual_revision_service.py:215-221 ───
[bug · medium] Robustness: `db.scalar(select(BagSceneLabelEvidence)...)` has no
`.order_by()`/`.limit(1)`. If two `MANUAL` rows ever exist for the same (bag_uid, scene_id, MANUAL)
— e.g. historical data, or the small race window above where two writers both observe no MANUAL row
before either commits — `scalar()` raises `MultipleResultsFound` (→500) instead of updating a
deterministic row. Add `.order_by(BagSceneLabelEvidence.id).limit(1)` (or `.desc()` for the latest)
so the effective projection always resolves to a single, well-defined row.



